WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:02.782
Welcome to SysNDD.

00:02.915 --> 00:05.961
Today, we explore phenotype patterns.

00:06.167 --> 00:12.223
In SysNDD, which phenotypes and
variant types are recorded together?

00:12.356 --> 00:16.966
Let's look at counts,
the correlogram, clusters and variants.

00:17.167 --> 00:21.405
Under Analyses, open Correlate phenotypes.

00:21.538 --> 00:25.568
The first three tabs use only Definitive entities

00:25.702 --> 00:28.280
with a neurodevelopmental phenotype,

00:28.413 --> 00:31.806
so their numbers differ from those under Tables.

00:32.033 --> 00:37.866
On Phenotype counts,
each bar is one phenotype, most frequent first.

00:38.000 --> 00:41.075
Its height is the number of entities with it.

00:41.209 --> 00:45.285
Select a bar to open them in Phenotype search.

00:45.500 --> 00:48.387
Now switch to Phenotype correlogram.

00:48.520 --> 00:53.025
Each cell compares two
phenotypes across the same entities.

00:53.159 --> 00:59.260
Red means a positive correlation,
blue a negative one, and white none.

00:59.467 --> 01:04.507
Hover over a cell,
and its value appears below the matrix.

01:04.733 --> 01:07.839
Here, Abnormality of the mitochondrion

01:07.972 --> 01:13.435
and Abnormality of the musculature
have a moderate positive correlation.

01:13.568 --> 01:17.853
The readout gives the value and how to read it.

01:18.067 --> 01:21.809
R is a Pearson correlation of annotations.

01:21.943 --> 01:24.098
It shows how closely two phenotypes

01:24.232 --> 01:27.768
go together in the curated entities, not why.

01:27.901 --> 01:31.747
An unrecorded phenotype counts as absent.

01:31.967 --> 01:34.723
Next comes Phenotype clustering,

01:34.856 --> 01:39.276
where SysNDD groups entities with similar phenotypes.

01:39.410 --> 01:43.803
Each bubble is one cluster, sized by its entities.

01:43.936 --> 01:47.376
When the page opens, one cluster is selected.

01:47.509 --> 01:52.526
Its organ-system involvement is
counted from the curated phenotypes.

01:52.733 --> 01:57.800
The input variables are
the phenotypes that define the clusters.

01:57.933 --> 02:00.962
A positive v-test means a phenotype is

02:01.095 --> 02:05.012
more common in the cluster than in all entities.

02:05.145 --> 02:09.693
Abnormality of the mitochondrion is strongly positive.

02:09.900 --> 02:14.055
A cluster is a computed group, not a diagnosis.

02:14.189 --> 02:17.831
The clusters are reproducible but weakly separated,

02:17.964 --> 02:21.023
and a later data version can regroup them.

02:21.157 --> 02:24.140
Read each one as a hypothesis.

02:24.367 --> 02:28.023
Under Analyses, select Correlate variants.

02:28.157 --> 02:31.580
Its terms come from the Variation Ontology.

02:31.714 --> 02:37.455
An out-of-frame indel, a frameshift,
correlates positively with truncation.

02:37.588 --> 02:41.967
That fits: frameshifts usually truncate the protein.

02:42.200 --> 02:45.499
At the top right, C-terminal protein elongation

02:45.632 --> 02:49.880
and protein elongation correlate perfectly.

02:50.100 --> 02:51.845
Select the cell.

02:51.978 --> 02:56.580
The Entities table lists
the Definitive entities behind it.

02:56.800 --> 03:01.614
Count them: a single entity
can produce a perfect correlation.

03:01.748 --> 03:04.000
That is not a pattern.

03:04.233 --> 03:06.827
Remember: count entities first.

03:06.961 --> 03:11.135
A correlation shows what
curators record together, not why.

03:11.268 --> 03:15.994
And a cluster is a hypothesis: check its entities.

03:16.200 --> 03:23.200
Find these views under Analyses,
at sysndd.dbmr.unibe.ch.

03:24.059 --> 03:29.238
Next, in C3: gene networks,
and where phenotypes meet them.
